WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:11.000
Permissions-Policy lets a site restrict access to browser features such as camera, microphone, geolocation, payment, fullscreen, sensors, and other capabilities.

00:00:11.220 --> 00:00:15.780
The purpose is to align browser capabilities with what the application actually needs.

00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:20.560
If a page does not need the microphone or location, the policy can say so.

00:00:20.780 --> 00:00:27.100
That reduces unnecessary exposure if a page, embedded component, or third-party script behaves unexpectedly.

00:00:27.320 --> 00:00:30.760
Feature controls are especially important in embedded contexts.

00:00:30.980 --> 00:00:38.180
An iframe may have its own allow attributes, and the top-level page may use Permissions-Policy to decide what embedded frames can request.

00:00:38.400 --> 00:00:44.400
This matters for partner content, payment flows, maps, media capture, and dashboard integrations.

00:00:44.620 --> 00:00:50.380
A permissive feature policy can accidentally grant capabilities to content that only needed to display information.

00:00:50.600 --> 00:00:53.480
The right policy starts with application requirements.

00:00:53.700 --> 00:00:56.900
A video conferencing page may need camera and microphone.

00:00:57.120 --> 00:00:59.520
A checkout flow may need payment features.

00:00:59.740 --> 00:01:02.700
A read-only help page probably needs neither.

00:01:02.920 --> 00:01:08.920
Policy design should be specific enough to support required functionality without enabling every browser feature everywhere.

00:01:09.140 --> 00:01:14.020
That specificity also helps reviewers understand why a feature is available on a page.

00:01:14.240 --> 00:01:17.760
Permissions-Policy should be maintained as the application changes.

00:01:17.980 --> 00:01:25.020
New product features, third-party embeds, browser changes, and platform migrations can all affect what capabilities are needed.

00:01:25.240 --> 00:01:32.440
As with CSP, the risk is cargo-cult configuration: copying a header that appears strict but does not match reality.

00:01:32.660 --> 00:01:38.340
Treat feature policy as part of the application contract and revisit it when pages gain new capabilities.
